Hello Make: team,

a workshop pitch that ends with something you can hold.

Take any downloaded handle with a knurled grip. In a mesh tool the knurl is a
carpet of hundreds of faces, and making the grooves deeper means redrawing the
part. In Solidon3D 0.5.0 the knurl is one feature in the object tree, with
pitch, cell width and depth. Change three numbers and it is reapplied. Because
a pattern on a cylinder is measured in the unrolled surface, the grooves are
equally deep all the way round and the pattern closes without a seam. Print
the original and the modified grip and you have a direct comparison of grip
and surface finish.

For context: Solidon3D sits between the downloaded model and the slicer. Click
a hole and its dimensions appear on the model, type 8 instead of 6 and the
countersink follows, pick a fit for the screw, read the check report, hand the
file to the slicer you already use. No CAD knowledge needed.

The more interesting half for a maker audience is what the part is afterwards:
still an exact body. Holes, countersinks and threads stay real faces and edges
through moving, duplicating and removing, and STEP goes in and comes back out
cleanly. That closes a gap that has been open for years, because CAD packages
accept an STL but cannot grip triangles, and the mesh tools that could edit
them are discontinued.

If the article involves a screw or a press fit, one detail is worth a
paragraph of its own. Clearance is not entered as a number but as a reference
to the material, so "clearance for PETG" rather than a value in millimetres.
Print the supplied tolerance ladder, measure it, set the profile, and every
fit in every project uses your printer's numbers from then on. Threads,
inserts, nut traps and magnet pockets come from standards tables rather than
from a guess.

The demo is free and complete, so nothing is needed from me to run the test. I
am happy to send print-resolution images or the models I used.

Solidon3D runs on Windows, macOS and Linux, in six languages, with no account
and no network.

